+// Did the path move at all between these two vertex indices, inclusive?
+//
+// The enabled list is full of holes, and 90% of runs were being cut by one:
+// 1372290 segments came out of the first working merge as 846372, an average
+// run of 1.62, with 758093 of those runs ended by a gap and only 464 by
+// geometry. The toolpath is collinear; the index list is shredded.
+//
+// Most of those holes have no spatial extent. Retract, Unretract and Seam are
+// move types emitted *at a point* - the nozzle does not move - and on top of
+// being options in their own right they invalidate the lines either side of
+// them, because extract_pos_and_or_hwa() only accepts a line whose two ends
+// share a move type. So one seam at the end of a perimeter loop removes three
+// consecutive indices while the extruder stands still, and a model with a loop
+// per island per layer has hundreds of thousands of them.
+//
+// Stepping over a hole like that is exact rather than approximate: if every
+// skipped vertex sits at the same place, the merged chord passes through the
+// identical set of points and no line is drawn that was not drawn before. A
+// hidden *travel* has real extent and fails this test, so the one thing that
+// must never happen - a chord painted across open air where the nozzle moved
+// without extruding - cannot.
+bool ViewerImpl::zero_extent_span(uint32_t from, uint32_t to) const
+{
+    if (to >= static_cast<uint32_t>(m_vertices.size()))
+        return false;
+    const Vec3& at = m_vertices[from].position;
+    for (uint32_t k = from + 1; k <= to; ++k) {
+        if (m_vertices[k].position != at)
+            return false;
+    }
+    return true;
+}
